Once you have RedBoot on your device, you can install OpenWrt Linux kernel and root filesystem:

<code>
load -r -b 0x00080000 -h 192.168.1.2 openwrt-24.10.0-ixp4xx-generic-usrobotics_usr8200-squashfs-kernel.bin
fis unlock linux
fis write -f 0x50180000 -b 0x00080000 -l 0x0033791e
fis lock linux
load -r -b 0x00080000 -h 192.168.1.2 openwrt-24.10.0-ixp4xx-generic-usrobotics_usr8200-squashfs-rootfs.bin
fis unlock rootfs
fis write -f 0x50580000 -b 0x00080000 -l 0x680000
fis lock rootfs
</code>

===== Booting Linux kernel from RAM =====

If you just want to boot a new kernel from RAM without flashing it first (such as for testing) you can do it like this:

<code>
load -r -b 0x00080000 -h 192.168.1.2 openwrt-24.10.0-ixp4xx-generic-usrobotics_usr8200-squashfs-kernel.bin
exec
</code>

